## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to implement a Trie with efficient prefix queries, which is fundamental for autocomplete features. The per-node counter design enables O(k) prefix counting where k is the prefix length, and O(m) insertion where m is the word length.

## Key Concepts

- Trie structure with per-node subtree counts
- Incrementing counts during insertion for O(1) prefix queries
- Prefix traversal and counting
- Edge case handling for empty strings, null inputs, and non-existent prefixes

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeCount = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (!word || typeof word !== 'string') {
      return;
    }

    let node = this.root;
    node.subtreeCount++;

    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeCount++;
    }

    node.isWord = true;
  }

  getPrefixCount(prefix) {
    if (!prefix || typeof prefix !== 'string') {
      return 0;
    }

    let node = this.root;

    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return 0;
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    return node.subtreeCount;
  }
}

// Example usage:
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('cat');
trie.insert('cater');
trie.insert('category');
trie.insert('dog');

console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('cat'));  // 3 (cat, cater, category)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('ca'));   // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('c'));    // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('dog'));  // 1
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('z'));    // 0 (non-existent prefix)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(''));     // 0 (empty string)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(null));   // 0 (null input)
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity**: O(m) for insert, O(k) for getPrefixCount where m is word length and k is prefix length
- **Space Complexity**: O(ALPHABET_SIZE × m × n) where m is average word length and n is number of words, but optimized with Map-based children

